feat(power): reset through the PMIC PON hard-reset path

Writing the PON reason and resetting with PSCI SYSTEM_RESET did not put
the board into fastboot.  The vendor's reboot bootloader path does not
use PSCI at all: msm-poweroff.c configures qpnp_pon_system_pwr_off() and
then calls deassert_ps_hold().  Add that path, keeping the reason write
and the IMEM cookie unchanged:

  - PS_HOLD_RST_CTL2 (0x85B) bit 7 is QPNP_PON_RESET_EN and
    PS_HOLD_RST_CTL (0x85A) low nibble is the power-off type.  Clear the
    enable, wait 500 us, write PON_POWER_OFF_TYPE_HARD_RESET (0x07) or
    ..._WARM_RESET (0x01), set the enable, and read both registers back;
  - deassert PS_HOLD by reading and then writing 0 to the TCSR register
    at 0x0C264000, the documented fallback of deassert_ps_hold() (this
    port has no SCM layer);
  - keep the vendor order: configure the reset, write the reason, write
    IMEM, then drop PS_HOLD.

Two new commands select the mechanism per test without a reflash:
FASTBOOT-PMIC (hard reset) and FASTBOOT-WARM (warm reset).  PON-RST
writes the same two registers, verifies them, then restores their old
values, so the reset configuration can be validated while the board can
still answer.  FASTBOOT keeps the PSCI reset, so the reason write and
the reset mechanism remain separate variables.

All PMIC and PS_HOLD writes stay behind kebab_pon_write=1, default off.

